1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,426
Every job description in this course has opened the same way: one sentence stating

2
00:00:05,426 --> 00:00:06,478
the task plainly.

3
00:00:06,478 --> 00:00:08,088
This one keeps that habit.

4
00:00:08,088 --> 00:00:12,668
The task is to work out which of three standard renewal documents, a stock

5
00:00:12,668 --> 00:00:17,496
valuation, a fire safety certificate, a signed renewal form, a client is still

6
00:00:17,496 --> 00:00:22,634
missing as their renewal date closes in, and say so before anyone has to go looking

7
00:00:22,634 --> 00:00:24,058
for the answer by hand.

8
00:00:24,058 --> 00:00:25,420
That is the whole job.

9
00:00:25,420 --> 00:00:30,495
What follows is the same six steps from 11.1, run once more end to end, so you can

10
00:00:30,495 --> 00:00:34,766
watch the method work on something new before you run it on your own.

11
00:00:34,766 --> 00:00:39,347
Nothing about this build is more advanced than what you have already done.

12
00:00:39,347 --> 00:00:44,175
It is the same habits, aimed one more time at Priya's paperwork instead of her

13
00:00:44,175 --> 00:00:44,547
inbox.

14
00:00:45,097 --> 00:00:50,318
Start with the sentence you always start with, because Section 1.4's discipline does

15
00:00:50,318 --> 00:00:53,178
not get a pass just for being a demonstration.

16
00:00:53,178 --> 00:00:55,602
Notice the verb: works out, not chases.

17
00:00:55,602 --> 00:01:00,513
That single word rules out half of what this build could quietly tempt you into

18
00:01:00,513 --> 00:01:04,553
adding, and it is worth reading back before you touch n8n at all.

19
00:01:04,553 --> 00:01:06,418
Scope stays narrow on purpose.

20
00:01:06,418 --> 00:01:11,391
One sheet, the pipeline you already met in Section 5, carrying three new columns

21
00:01:11,391 --> 00:01:13,878
Priya added herself for document status.

22
00:01:13,878 --> 00:01:19,037
Nothing wider, and nothing written back to it: this agent reads, it never edits the

23
00:01:19,037 --> 00:01:20,902
source of truth it depends on.

24
00:01:20,902 --> 00:01:26,061
Action follows straight from that: read only, output only, so there is nothing here

25
00:01:26,061 --> 00:01:27,864
for a mistaken click to send.

26
00:01:28,414 --> 00:01:32,107
The ceiling is where it is worth slowing down, same as always.

27
00:01:32,107 --> 00:01:34,907
This agent never decides a document has landed.

28
00:01:34,907 --> 00:01:39,553
It only repeats what the column already says, because a checklist that quietly

29
00:01:39,553 --> 00:01:41,936
starts guessing stops being a checklist.

30
00:01:41,936 --> 00:01:46,880
And it never emails a client to chase a missing signature, because that job already

31
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:51,526
belongs to Agent 4, built with its own draft-only guardrail back in Section 6.

32
00:01:51,526 --> 00:01:56,292
Two agents quietly doing the same job with two different safety records is worse

33
00:01:56,292 --> 00:02:01,295
than one agent doing it properly, and that is reason enough for you to leave chasing

34
00:02:01,295 --> 00:02:02,785
out of this one entirely.

35
00:02:02,785 --> 00:02:03,857
One week on trial.

36
00:02:03,857 --> 00:02:08,861
Every flagged row checked by eye each morning against the sheet itself: short enough

37
00:02:08,861 --> 00:02:13,626
to actually happen, long enough to catch a pattern rather than a fluke, the same

38
00:02:13,626 --> 00:02:16,664
balance every trial task in this course has struck.

39
00:02:17,214 --> 00:02:22,088
Add a Schedule Trigger first, set to run every morning rather than Agent 3's

40
00:02:22,088 --> 00:02:23,307
once-a-week rhythm.

41
00:02:23,307 --> 00:02:28,438
Documents shift daily as a renewal date closes in, and a Monday-only check would

42
00:02:28,438 --> 00:02:30,554
miss most of the week's movement.

43
00:02:30,554 --> 00:02:35,493
After it, add a Google Sheets node, operation Get Row(s), pointed at the same

44
00:02:35,493 --> 00:02:37,674
renewal pipeline from Section 5.2.

45
00:02:37,674 --> 00:02:41,329
The only real change is what is sitting in the sheet now.

46
00:02:41,329 --> 00:02:46,589
Alongside Client, Premium, Renewal Date and Status sit three more columns, one for

47
00:02:46,589 --> 00:02:51,976
each document, each one either blank or marked Received the moment Priya's assistant

48
00:02:51,976 --> 00:02:52,489
logs it.

49
00:02:52,489 --> 00:02:57,877
Pin a handful of rows here the way Section 5.2 did, so you can see real data flowing

50
00:02:57,877 --> 00:03:00,057
before you write a word of prompt.

51
00:03:00,057 --> 00:03:05,445
Attach an AI Agent node after the Sheets node, model connected the same way as every

52
00:03:05,445 --> 00:03:06,471
build before it.

53
00:03:06,471 --> 00:03:11,666
The Prompt field hands the model every row the sheet just read, seven columns per

54
00:03:11,666 --> 00:03:13,783
client, nothing filtered out yet.

55
00:03:13,783 --> 00:03:19,170
What happens to that data next is entirely down to one paragraph you write yourself,

56
00:03:19,170 --> 00:03:21,543
and that paragraph is the next slide.

57
00:03:22,093 --> 00:03:24,987
Look at what that paragraph is actually doing.

58
00:03:24,987 --> 00:03:26,685
One sentence sets the role.

59
00:03:26,685 --> 00:03:29,831
One sentence names exactly which columns to trust.

60
00:03:29,831 --> 00:03:34,863
One sentence sets the window, fourteen days, not fifty, not one: a number chosen

61
00:03:34,863 --> 00:03:39,958
because it gives Priya enough runway to actually chase something before a renewal

62
00:03:39,958 --> 00:03:40,399
lapses.

63
00:03:40,399 --> 00:03:45,305
And the last sentence is the ceiling from the job description, restated in the

64
00:03:45,305 --> 00:03:50,212
model's own instructions rather than left to sit unused on a page nobody opens

65
00:03:50,212 --> 00:03:50,589
again.

66
00:03:50,589 --> 00:03:53,231
That repetition is deliberate, not sloppy.

67
00:03:53,231 --> 00:03:58,515
A ceiling written once in C05 and never mentioned again is a ceiling the model never

68
00:03:58,515 --> 00:03:59,459
actually reads.

69
00:03:59,459 --> 00:04:04,428
Say it twice: once for the human filing the job description, once for the model

70
00:04:04,428 --> 00:04:05,309
doing the job.

71
00:04:05,859 --> 00:04:11,305
Add a Switch node after the agent, the same shape as Agent 1's build in Section 3.4.

72
00:04:11,305 --> 00:04:14,611
One rule: does the answer contain the word MISSING.

73
00:04:14,611 --> 00:04:19,344
Send that exit to a node named plainly, Flag for Priya, documents needed.

74
00:04:19,344 --> 00:04:24,466
Everything else falls through to a second node named just as plainly, Clear, no

75
00:04:24,466 --> 00:04:25,373
action needed.

76
00:04:25,373 --> 00:04:29,134
Both are placeholders, exactly the way Section 3.4's were.

77
00:04:29,134 --> 00:04:34,515
The job description says output only, so neither node sends anything or touches the

78
00:04:34,515 --> 00:04:34,904
sheet.

79
00:04:34,904 --> 00:04:40,026
They exist so a stranger reading this canvas in six months knows what each path

80
00:04:40,026 --> 00:04:45,212
means without asking you, which is the whole point of naming a node the way this

81
00:04:45,212 --> 00:04:46,898
course keeps insisting on.

82
00:04:47,448 --> 00:04:52,271
Pin this against a test date of 1 September and run it once, live, before a single

83
00:04:52,271 --> 00:04:54,153
real call goes anywhere near it.

84
00:04:54,153 --> 00:04:58,918
Brightwell sits exactly fourteen days out, right at the edge of the window rather

85
00:04:58,918 --> 00:05:03,623
than comfortably inside it, and the agent still catches its missing signed form.

86
00:05:03,623 --> 00:05:05,035
That is not an accident.

87
00:05:05,035 --> 00:05:09,623
A boundary case like that is exactly what pinned test data is for: proving the

88
00:05:09,623 --> 00:05:12,858
ceiling holds at the edge, not just in the easy middle.

89
00:05:12,858 --> 00:05:17,622
Fenwick sits eight days out with two documents still missing, and the agent names

90
00:05:17,622 --> 00:05:22,092
both rather than saying something vague like "some documents outstanding." A

91
00:05:22,092 --> 00:05:26,857
checklist that makes Priya go and look again has not actually saved her anything.

92
00:05:26,857 --> 00:05:29,504
Oakfield is the row worth staring at longest.

93
00:05:29,504 --> 00:05:33,092
All three documents blank, and the agent says nothing at all.

94
00:05:33,092 --> 00:05:33,327
Why?

95
00:05:33,327 --> 00:05:38,209
Because the thirtieth of September is more than fourteen days from the pinned date,

96
00:05:38,209 --> 00:05:42,914
and the system message explicitly says to ignore anything further out than that.

97
00:05:42,914 --> 00:05:47,796
An agent that flagged every blank cell regardless of date would bury Priya in noise

98
00:05:47,796 --> 00:05:49,384
a month before it mattered.

99
00:05:49,384 --> 00:05:54,325
One that only flags what is actually close is doing the job the description actually

100
00:05:54,325 --> 00:05:54,913
asked for.

101
00:05:55,463 --> 00:06:00,396
That is the whole build: six ordinary steps, and nothing here needed a technique you

102
00:06:00,396 --> 00:06:03,566
had not already used somewhere earlier in this course.

103
00:06:03,566 --> 00:06:08,264
The trigger is Section 5's, the judgement is Section 3's, the routing is Section

104
00:06:08,264 --> 00:06:10,906
3.4's, borrowed and pointed at a new problem.

105
00:06:10,906 --> 00:06:15,721
Next lecture picks up exactly here: a real week of trial, the guardrails worksheet

106
00:06:15,721 --> 00:06:20,125
filled in honestly, and the actual number this agent would cost you to run.
